{"sourceSha256": "dd6395865da5731c4f231c551f344844246bd90fb3ad9a36b77e92b691ee87b3", "text": "Ticket Id: 24720\n\nDr Lisa Smith\n\nEnocrinologist\n\nCity Hospital\n\nNewtown\n\n10/02/2014\n\nDear Dr. Smith,\n\nRE: Mrs Priya Sharma                              DOB: 8/05/1953\n\nThank you for seeing Mrs Sharma, a 60- year old retired woman. Your further management to control her\nblood glucose level would be highly appreciated.\n\nMrs Sharma has a medical history of NIDDM for 20 years. Metformin and glipizide has been used to control         Commented [JG1]: have\nblood sugar level. She is known to be allergic to pencillin. Please note that she has strong family history of   Commented [JG2]: her\ndiabetes mellitus.                                                                                               Commented [JG3]: penicillin\n\nOn 29/12/2013, Mrs Sharma attended the health centre to check her blood glucose level. Recent glucose\nlevel was high about 6 to 18 mmol/l. Her blood pressure was 155/100. Her medical condition regarding eye\nexamination, weight, bowel and nutrition was good. Some tests were required and Atacand was added.\n\n2 weeks later, pathology report revealed poor control of blood sugar proved by high HbA1c and high\ncholesterol. Other laboratory results were normal. Accordingly, atorvastatin 20 mg was added and\nmetformin dose changed from 2 nocte to 1 bd. At the end of that month, blood pressure and glucose level\nimproved.\n\nToday, lipid profile showed improvement as the cholesterol decreased from 6.2 to 3.2, LDLC 1.1 and\ntriglycerides decreased from 2.4 to 1.7.\n\nIn view of the above, I am referring Mrs Sharma for your further assessment and control of her diabetes.\n\nFor any queries, please contact me.\n\nYours sincerely,\n\ndoctor\n\n                                     Report\n Word length                  213\n Comments                     Good job! The letter is carefully written. The\n                              syntax and lexis are accurate, and the history is\n                              explained in a clear, thorough and logical manner\n Estimated Grade              A\n Advice                        1. Always proofread your letter\n                               2. Keep up the good work!\n Helpful links                https://www.benchmarkedu.com.au/oet-reading-\n                              practice-tests\n                              https://www.benchmarkedu.com.au/oet-writing-\n                              correction\n                              https://www.facebook.com/groups/oethelp\n\n\n\n\n296 Henley Beach Rd, Underdale, 5032\nhttp://www.benchmarkedu.com.au/oet-writing-correction\nwww.benchmarkedu.com.au/pteoetielts", "pages": [{"number": 1, "text": "Ticket Id: 24720\n\nDr Lisa Smith\n\nEnocrinologist\n\nCity Hospital\n\nNewtown\n\n10/02/2014\n\nDear Dr. Smith,\n\nRE: Mrs Priya Sharma                              DOB: 8/05/1953\n\nThank you for seeing Mrs Sharma, a 60- year old retired woman. Your further management to control her\nblood glucose level would be highly appreciated.\n\nMrs Sharma has a medical history of NIDDM for 20 years. Metformin and glipizide has been used to control         Commented [JG1]: have\nblood sugar level. She is known to be allergic to pencillin. Please note that she has strong family history of   Commented [JG2]: her\ndiabetes mellitus.                                                                                               Commented [JG3]: penicillin\n\nOn 29/12/2013, Mrs Sharma attended the health centre to check her blood glucose level. Recent glucose\nlevel was high about 6 to 18 mmol/l. Her blood pressure was 155/100. Her medical condition regarding eye\nexamination, weight, bowel and nutrition was good. Some tests were required and Atacand was added.\n\n2 weeks later, pathology report revealed poor control of blood sugar proved by high HbA1c and high\ncholesterol. Other laboratory results were normal. Accordingly, atorvastatin 20 m